{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"ea9b6a017fad5834f1ba855584a04e144af19af8dd9966765be2f5ae997c70dc","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_b6d1cd76bf95183b1e0ee575850b4fd9bcc0249c53446741266a47b27e76a074","overall_assessment":"The paper addresses a significant challenge in machine learning by extending the Learning to Optimize framework to high-dimensional stochastic problems, specifically for training shallow neural networks. The work demonstrates that a learned optimization algorithm can generalize across different datasets and architectures, outperforming standard hand-engineered methods. This contributes to the broader goal of automating the design of optimization tools, a core component of the machine learning pipeline.","paper_id":"BkM27IxR-","paper_identity_sha256":"8c4854976b0623963fc98c658d82900346a27ea4fae7aea67be8aabaeaf9f0cc","prompt_identity_sha256":"03ad2d21f999054865701ff20f4c61174ed966d7be551ca06fcd96e5e6e54520","raw_response_sha256":"2838986b99cb47c8990fceda2daf74267c2c33d33a79a1e55c202eb96f21862f","request_identity_sha256":"5bace63fd05bd3dc7124ecd58f24c862430111ef3faa98e630377609706382b2","reviewer_identity_sha256":"c1cd7c7fdd4a2cbeb45c2f21af982ad0f30bffae8ae148e3eba36479685cf360","role":"SIGN","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"6a550b1f09993ce9bfdd2db2c653b409a1eb1f2a0435327cac57b08548a3e881","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-002","SRC-003","SRC-010"],"review_text":"The work addresses a broadly important problem by targeting the design of optimization algorithms, which are fundamental tools in machine learning. The paper argues that the design of these tools has been left to human experts and proposes a data-driven approach to learn them. By extending the Learning to Optimize framework to high-dimensional stochastic problems, the paper tackles a challenge that is relevant to a wide range of machine learning applications, potentially influencing research in optimization, reinforcement learning, and meta-learning.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-BRD"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-001","SRC-034","SRC-036"],"review_text":"The paper demonstrates domain impact by applying the learned optimization algorithm to the training of shallow neural networks on standard computer vision datasets (MNIST, TFD, CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100). The method is shown to outperform popular hand-engineered algorithms like ADAM, AdaGrad, and RMSprop in these specific settings. The focus on shallow neural nets and specific datasets limits the immediate domain applicability to deep learning or other specialized fields, but it provides a concrete demonstration of the method's utility in a common machine learning task.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-DOM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-036","SRC-037","SRC-039","SRC-040"],"review_text":"The paper reports that the learned optimization algorithm (Predicted Step Descent) consistently outperforms seven hand-engineered baselines (SGD, momentum, conjugate gradient, L-BFGS, ADAM, AdaGrad, RMSprop) and a learned baseline from Andrychowicz et al. (2016) across multiple datasets and architectures. The improvements are described as consistent, with the learned algorithm descending to the optimum the fastest. The paper also demonstrates robustness to changes in stochasticity (mini-batch size) and architecture (number of parameters), showing that the learned algorithm maintains its performance advantage in these varied conditions.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-SOT"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-002","SRC-003"],"review_text":"The paper does not explicitly discuss the real-world or societal impact of the learned optimization algorithm. It focuses on the technical performance and generalization capabilities of the method. While the potential to automate the design of optimization tools could have practical benefits for machine learning practitioners, the paper does not address who benefits from this automation, potential risks, or the environmental impact of using learned versus hand-engineered optimizers. The lack of discussion on these aspects limits the assessment of its real-world and societal significance.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-IMP"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"f61caf314d806c8602d8cc2d2bec8ef5896c0e9cd20d22333a22d8651d83b7fb","paper_id":"BkM27IxR-","paper_ordinal":97,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"2838986b99cb47c8990fceda2daf74267c2c33d33a79a1e55c202eb96f21862f","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"BkM27IxR-","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"309571c912bb72a49f7db5a8ff0d21be0fba18a3eeb2772dcdae8f9aa09c8d77","request_identity_sha256":"5bace63fd05bd3dc7124ecd58f24c862430111ef3faa98e630377609706382b2","role":"SIGN","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v5","usage":{"completion_tokens":806,"prompt_tokens":18342,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":19148},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"5bace63fd05bd3dc7124ecd58f24c862430111ef3faa98e630377609706382b2","role":"SIGN","role_ordinal":2,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}